     { id: 'two-database-logins', says: 'Two logins to the database, never one. The application connects as content_app, which cannot UPDATE, DELETE or TRUNCATE the log; the migration runs as the owner, over a URL the application is never given.', why: 'One login that can SET ROLE can RESET ROLE, and a revoke it can undo proves nothing.' },
     { id: 'uploads-never-overwrite', says: 'Files are never overwritten: every upload is a new object.', why: 'A version that was signed keeps pointing at the bytes it was signed on.' },
     { id: 'one-statement-per-page', says: 'A list reads in one statement to the database per page, whatever the page holds, on snapshots the running build wrote. A snapshot an older build left is replayed and written back once, apart from the page and outside its count, so the first list after a deploy may cost more and every list after it costs one.', why: 'A stale snapshot replayed on the read path costs more than one statement a document, and a deploy step that rebuilds every snapshot is a step nobody asked for.' },
+  { id: 'design-scan-before-ready', says: 'Before a ticket that touches a wireframe is READY, ds-scan.js runs over every drawing and its findings are zero or written into the ticket. Each of its rules reads its scale from the tokens rather than from a list in the scan, so moving a token moves the rule, and the shape rule reports a shape three or more files draw and no row claims. The same rules run over the app’s components: one set of rules, two places that read it. An exemption is written in the scan, never in a script that calls it, and covers a marked range, not a whole file; the files out of scope are named in the scan with their reason. A sweep runs over the same list the rule claims, the count is read per file, and a file is read again after a sweep.', why: 'A value picked by eye drifts the day it is drawn, an exemption is where drift hides, and a sweep over the files a tool can open is not a sweep over the files the rule covers.' },
   ],
 };
 
